Docker access is fixed and A3 is now genuinely verified, not just written. Summary:

What happened: Docker was installed but the socket needed docker group membership, which you granted via sudo usermod. Since that doesn't apply to my already-running shell, I'm using sg docker -c "..." for the rest of this session to pick up the new group.

Testing the script for real caught 3 actual bugs no amount of reading docs would have found:

nsc list ... --json writes its JSON to stderr, not stdout — my idempotency check was discarding stderr, so it always saw "not found" and always tried to re-add, which is exactly why a second run failed with operator ... exists already.
nsc's table output carries ANSI color codes even without a TTY, sitting flush against the name with no word boundary — grep -w silently never matched. Switched every existence check to --json output instead.
jq, which I'd assumed was present on the host as a "standard utility," actually wasn't installed. Rather than ask for yet another native install, I rewrote the JSON extraction to go through the already-containerized yq alone.
There was also a real command-syntax error caught before it ever ran — the earlier draft used a --operator-jwt flag on nsc generate config that doesn't exist; the operator JWT turns out to be embedded directly in the generated resolver.conf rather than written separately.

Verified outcome: 3 consecutive full runs, all 6 adapter identities created with correct permissions, .creds files generated for each, resolver.conf generated with the operator JWT and GSB account JWT preloaded — and confirmed no duplicate permission entries accumulate across reruns. Confirmed git status shows none of the generated secrets (infra/nats/operator/, resolver.conf) as tracked — .gitignore is doing its job.

Design.md §11, the README, and Dependencies.md are all updated to reflect what was actually verified rather than what was hoped to work. A3 is genuinely done now, not just written. Ready for A4 (nats-server.conf, wiring in resolver.conf) whenever you want to continue.

