Then went past "did it create the objects" to verify the actual mechanism Decision #7 depends on: wrote two keys to the real bucket, refreshed only one at t=30s, checked both at t=65s. The refreshed key was still alive (revision 3); the unrefreshed one had genuinely expired. That's the heartbeat/TTL registry pattern confirmed working, not just configured.

One nuance worth remembering: NATS's --ttl here is a bucket-wide max-age keyed off each entry's own last-write time, not the separate "Per-Key TTL" feature (--marker-ttl) meant for different TTLs per key. Since our design only ever needs one uniform value, the simpler mechanism was the right (and sufficient) choice — noted in Design.md so "per-key TTL" language elsewhere isn't misread as a claim about that other feature.

