20 passed in 0.09s
B3 done — and it caught two real problems, one of which required going back and fixing B2.

What's built: crypto.py — encryption via pyrage (encrypt_for_recipients/decrypt), Ed25519 signing via nkeys (sign/verify), both digest-based (SHA-256, centralized in one _digest() function so signer and verifier can never disagree about what gets signed).

Two real findings, both from actually running the libraries, not from reading their docs:

Had to go back and fix B2's schema. age bundles all per-recipient key-wrapping inside its single ciphertext — confirmed by actually encrypting for two recipients and inspecting the output (377 bytes of opaque age format, no separable per-recipient data). My B2 schema's recipients: list[{keyId, wrappedKey}] assumed a hand-rolled envelope structure that doesn't correspond to how age actually works. Fixed to recipients: list[str] — envelope.py, its tests, and Design.md §5's example JSON all updated to match. This is the kind of mismatch that pure schema design genuinely can't catch on its own; it only showed up once real crypto had to conform to it.

Found and worked around a real bug in nkeys.py 0.2.1. KeyPair.verify() only works if you already hold the private key — confirmed by reading its source, not guessing. There's no working path to verify a signature with just someone's public key, which defeats the entire point of asymmetric signatures and is exactly the scenario Decision #5 depends on (a recipient verifying a sender they never had private key material from). Worked around with a small, independently-tested decode of nkey's public-key encoding feeding nacl.signing.VerifyKey directly — verified byte-for-byte correct against real generated keys, with a dedicated test that deliberately keeps only the public key in scope, so a regression here can't hide behind a test that still happens to have the private seed available.

Verified: 20 tests total, all passing (10 new), plus clean ruff, mypy (needed a standard per-module override once nkeys/pyrage turned out to ship no type stubs), bandit, pip-audit, and the full pre-commit chain. Dependencies.md updated with real resolved versions, including PyNaCl — which turned out to already be in the tree transitively via nkeys, and is now doing real work in the verification workaround rather than sitting unused as the documented fallback.

Ready for B4 (config.py — the pydantic-settings loader for adapter config) whenever you want to continue.

