B4 done — verified for real, with one more genuine library-behavior surprise caught by actually running it.

What's built: config.py — AdapterSettings, a pydantic-settings base class matching §7.6's env vars exactly (GSB_SERVICE_ID, GSB_NATS_URL, GSB_NATS_CREDS_PATH, GSB_SUBJECTS, GSB_LOG_LEVEL), designed to be subclassed per adapter for its own additional settings. nats_creds_path uses pydantic's FilePath type, so a missing .creds file fails immediately at config-load time rather than as a confusing connection error later.

One clarification worth flagging: §7.6 only ever sketched GSB_SUBJECTS as a name, no real definition. I settled it as "subjects this instance subscribes to / registers as a recipient for" — not publish subjects, since what an adapter publishes in response to what is inherent to its own code, not something you'd want to change via env var without also changing logic.

The real finding: pydantic-settings attempts its own JSON-decode of list-typed fields at the settings-source layer, before any field_validator runs — I'd written a mode="before" validator specifically to accept comma-separated input, and it silently never got invoked; the library errored out first with a low-level SettingsError. Fixed with Annotated[list[str], NoDecode], which defers entirely to the validator. Comma-separated support was worth keeping deliberately — GSB_SUBJECTS=events.a,events.b is far more natural to write in a docker-compose.yml environment: block than JSON, which needs careful shell-quoting.

Verified: 9 new tests (29 total), clean ruff, mypy, bandit, pip-audit, and the full pre-commit chain. Dependencies.md updated with real resolved versions, including python-dotenv, pulled in transitively for .env file support.

Ready for B5 (registry.py — the KV client wrapper) whenever you want to continue. That's the first Track B piece that needs a live NATS, so I'll be testing it against the real A6 stack, same as Track A.

