B5 done — the most surprising step yet, and every surprise came from actually running it, not from reading nats-py's API.

What's built: registry.py — RegistryClient (register/heartbeat/deregister, one-shot lookup) and RecipientCache (a live, watch-updated view — the "JetStream KV watch, not a GET per publish" pattern Design.md §4.5 calls for).

Five real findings, all confirmed by testing against the live A6 stack, none of them guessable from the API shape alone:

KeyValue.keys(filters=...) looks like a NATS wildcard filter and isn't one. Reading nats-py's source revealed it's a plain Python substring check applied client-side, after fetching everything via an unfiltered watch. Every wildcard pattern I tried (*, >, combinations) failed with NoKeysError — a literal prefix string worked, because it's a genuine substring, not a wildcard match.
KeyValue.watch(keys=...), in contrast, genuinely does use its argument as a real NATS subject — confirmed by watching a wildcard pattern and verifying a concurrent put to an unrelated key never arrived.
A watch's first item, and every subsequent "you're caught up" point, is None — not a real entry, and it's nats-py's own internal convention.
updates(timeout=...) raises on idle timeout rather than returning None — easy to conflate with finding #3 if you're not careful; they needed different handling in the consume loop.
TTL-based expiry arrives as operation == KV_PURGE, distinct from explicit KV_DEL — both needed handling for RecipientCache to self-heal when an adapter stops heartbeating. Verified directly, not assumed: registered a recipient, watched the cache pick it up, deregistered it, watched the cache empty out again through the watch, not a re-poll.
There was also one genuinely confusing near-miss along the way — an early test failed with NoKeysError that turned out to be the bucket's own 60s TTL naturally expiring test keys between separate tool calls, not a bug. Worth remembering since it'll look identical to a real failure if you're not watching the clock.

