B7 done — and with it, Phase 1 is complete.

What's built: test_end_to_end.py — FakePublisherAdapter/FakeConsumerAdapter, small classes that model the actual shape real Phase 2 adapters will take (connect once at startup, then run an ongoing loop) rather than a linear test script calling BusClient directly. Two tests: a single event end-to-end, and a sequence of three, with the two adapters running concurrently via asyncio.gather — not sequential awaits — since that's what B6's tests hadn't specifically proven. Also refactored shared test setup out of test_bus_client.py into _helpers.py/conftest.py so both files share one source of truth.

One more real finding along the way: an early version of the multi-event test used three separate FakePublisherAdapter instances sharing one serviceId, connecting concurrently — 2 of 3 messages failed signature verification. Not a bug in the system: the test helper generates a fresh random signing key per connection for convenience, and since identity registration is last-writer-wins per serviceId (§4.6), three concurrent connections kept overwriting each other's trusted key. A real adapter never does this — one serviceId means one persistent key from one .creds file, and horizontal scaling means separate serviceIds per replica (Decision #18), not sharing one. Fixed by using one publisher connection for the whole sequence — also just the more realistic shape.

The actual completion checkpoint: full teardown (docker compose down -v, wiped all generated auth state) and rebuild via up.sh from absolute scratch, then all 52 tests across every Track A and B step run against that genuinely fresh infra — all passing. Not "the last step passed" but "the whole thing rebuilt from nothing still works."

Phase 1 is done. Both tracks — A1–A7 (infra: toolchain, auth, server, JetStream objects, compose, smoke test) and B1–B7 (gsb-core: envelope, crypto, config, registry, bus client, end-to-end proof) — built and empirically verified against real infrastructure throughout, with every genuine bug and design gap found along the way fixed and documented rather than glossed over (the nkeys.py verification bug, the age/pyrage schema correction, several nats-py KV quirks, the sender-authentication gap and its resolution, and more).

Design.md now reflects Phase 1 as complete, with a pointer to what's next: §10 Phase 2 — the Webhook Listener → File Storage Adapter vertical slice, the first real adapters built on this foundation. Want to continue there, or pause here now that Phase 1's a clean stopping point?

