Dependencies.md:
14:## 1. Core Bus Library (`gsb-core`)
18:| `nats-py` | [PyPI](https://pypi.org/project/nats-py/) / [GitHub](https://github.com/nats-io/nats.py) | **2.15.0** (resolved via `uv add "nats-py[nkeys]"`) | Official async NATS/JetStream client; also provides the `[nkeys]` extra used for signing. Added in Step B3 to get `nkeys`; the client itself is exercised starting [registry.py](libs/gsb-core/gsb_core/registry.py) (§11 Step B5). | Official `nats-io` org project (backed by Synadia); same trust tier as the NATS server itself. Ships a `py.typed` marker (unlike `nkeys`/`pyrage`), but not every method is annotated — `KeyWatcher.updates` needed a narrow `type: ignore[no-untyped-call]`. **KV API has real surprises, confirmed by testing, not guessable from the API shape**: `KeyValue.keys(filters=...)` looks like a NATS wildcard filter but is actually a client-side Python substring check on already-fetched keys — a literal `*`/`>` never matches anything there. `KeyValue.watch(keys=...)`, by contrast, genuinely does use its argument as a NATS subject with real server-side filtering. See §11 Step B5 for the full list (5 findings). | 2026-08-24 |
19:| `nkeys` | [PyPI](https://pypi.org/project/nkeys/) / [GitHub](https://github.com/nats-io/nkeys.py) | **0.2.1** (resolved via `uv add`) | Ed25519 keypair generation/signing for both NATS connection auth and event signing (§4.1). Installed via `nats-py[nkeys]`. Used in [crypto.py](libs/gsb-core/gsb_core/crypto.py) (§11 Step B3). | Official `nats-io` org project. Implements NATS's own key format (not custom crypto). **Confirmed real bug** (not assumed): `KeyPair.verify()` only works with the private key already loaded — there's no working way to verify a signature from just a public key, defeating the actual point of asymmetric verification. Read the library's source to confirm, then worked around in `crypto.py` with a from-scratch, tested decode of the public-key encoding feeding `nacl.signing.VerifyKey` directly. Worth flagging upstream; not filed as of this writing. | 2026-08-24 |
20:| `pydantic` (v2) | [PyPI](https://pypi.org/project/pydantic/) | **2.13.4** (resolved via `uv sync`) | Event envelope ([envelope.py](libs/gsb-core/gsb_core/envelope.py), §11 Step B2), config, and adapter payload schema modeling/validation. | Extremely widely adopted (FastAPI's foundation); large maintainer team, active CVE monitoring history. Verified: 10 tests pass including exact wire-shape fidelity to §5; note the mypy plugin (`pydantic.mypy`, now enabled in root `pyproject.toml`) doesn't understand `populate_by_name=True` — confirmed by testing, see Decision-adjacent note at Step B2. | 2026-08-24 |
21:| `pydantic-settings` | [PyPI](https://pypi.org/project/pydantic-settings/) | **2.15.0** (resolved via `uv add`) | Env-var/`.env`-driven adapter configuration loading — [config.py](libs/gsb-core/gsb_core/config.py) (§11 Step B4). | Maintained by the Pydantic org itself; same trust tier as `pydantic`. **Confirmed real behavior** (not assumed): it attempts its own JSON-decode of list-typed env values *before* any `field_validator` runs, at the settings-source layer — a plain comma-separated value fails there outright. Worked around with `Annotated[list[str], NoDecode]`, deferring to a custom validator instead; see §11 Step B4. | 2026-08-24 |
23:| `pyrage` | [PyPI](https://pypi.org/project/pyrage/) / [background](https://blog.yossarian.net/2022/07/25/age-encryption-in-python-with-pyrage) | **1.4.0** (resolved via `uv add`) | Python bindings (via PyO3) to `rage`, the Rust implementation of the [age](https://age-encryption.org) format — provides the multi-recipient hybrid encryption in §4.3. Used in [crypto.py](libs/gsb-core/gsb_core/crypto.py) (§11 Step B3). | The cryptography itself is implemented in vetted Rust (`rage`/`age` core), not reimplemented in Python — bindings only. PyPI releases use Trusted Publishing (supply-chain provenance signal). Smaller install base than mainstream crypto libs; **recommendation:** re-check for an independent security review of `rage` before production use. **Adoption confirmed working as designed**, with one real schema implication: age bundles all per-recipient key-wrapping inside its ciphertext (verified by inspecting real encrypt output), which is why `envelope.py`'s `recipients` field changed to a flat key-string list — see §11 Step B3. `pip-audit`: no known vulnerabilities. | 2026-08-24 |
24:| `PyNaCl` | [PyPI](https://pypi.org/project/PyNaCl/) | **1.6.2** (pulled in transitively by `nkeys` — not directly declared) | libsodium bindings. Originally the documented fallback if `pyrage` integration proved impractical (§7.2) — turned out to be in the dependency tree anyway, just via `nkeys` rather than as gsb-core's own choice. Now doing real work in [crypto.py](libs/gsb-core/gsb_core/crypto.py): `nacl.signing.VerifyKey` is used directly to work around the `nkeys.py` verification bug noted above. | Long-established, widely audited (wraps libsodium). `pip-audit`: no known vulnerabilities. | 2026-08-24 |
39:| `uuid6` | [PyPI](https://pypi.org/project/uuid6/) | **2025.0.1** (resolved via `uv sync`) | `uuid7()` for event IDs — stdlib `uuid` doesn't gain native support until Python 3.14 (project targets 3.12, Decision #8). Wrapped by `new_event_id()` in [envelope.py](libs/gsb-core/gsb_core/envelope.py) (§11 Step B2). | Small, single-purpose implementation of a published RFC (RFC 9562); low risk surface. Verified: generates genuinely unique, correctly time-ordered ids (tested). | 2026-08-24 |
48:| `pytest`, `pytest-asyncio`, `pytest-cov` | [PyPI](https://pypi.org/project/pytest/) | **9.1.1 / 1.4.0 / 7.1.0** (resolved via `uv sync`, `uv.lock`) | Test framework + async support + coverage. | De facto standard Python test tooling. Verified: `libs/gsb-core`'s scaffold test passes. | 2026-08-24 |
