# Jet Core Service Bus

**Ongoing security validation process:**
- New dependency, at adoption time: check maintenance activity (recent releases/commits), check for known CVEs (PyPI advisory DB / OSV), check adoption/provenance signals (org-backed vs. single maintainer, download counts), check license compatibility.
Renamed to Jet Core Service Bus, verified end to end.

What changed, mechanically:

Title/prose: "Gregor's Service Bus" → "Jet Core Service Bus" across README.md, Design.md, Dependencies.md, and every infra script's header comments.
Package: libs/gsb-core → libs/jetcore, Python module gsb_core → jetcore (directory move + import rewrite across every .py file, tests included).
Env var prefix: GSB_ → JETCORE_ in config.py and its tests.
NATS identity layer (bootstrap_auth.sh): Operator GregorsServiceBus → JetCoreServiceBus, Account GSB → JETCORE, the gsb-admin provisioning identity → jetcore-admin (also updated in bootstrap_jetstream.sh, up.sh, adapter_identities.yaml).
Compose project: name: gsb → name: jetcore in docker-compose.yml (container is now jetcore-nats-1, network jetcore_default).
Root project name: gregors-service-bus → jet-core-service-bus; uv.lock regenerated to match both renamed packages.
Since the Operator/Account names are baked into the generated JWT/nkey store, this wasn't just a text rename — I tore down the old gsb Compose project and its stale gsb-nats-1 container/volume/network, wiped the generated operator/ auth state, and ran infra/nats/up.sh fresh to rebootstrap everything under the new identity names. Then re-ran the full toolchain against it: uv sync --all-packages, 52 tests × 3 (all passing), ruff, mypy, bandit, pip-audit — all clean. Test artifacts (stream messages, both KV buckets) purged afterward; jetcore-nats-1 left running and healthy.

One thing left as-is: the checkout directory itself is still named GregorsServiceBus — renaming that would move the folder out from under this session's cwd and your open editor, so I left it for you to do separately if you want it. Compose already pins its project/network name explicitly so it doesn't depend on the folder name either way.

git status --short shows the diff as modified files plus D/?? for the gsb-core→jetcore directory move (git will read that as a rename once you git add) — nothing staged or committed, per your standing instruction.

